// Kanji Phonetics data — kanji grouped by shared phonetic component ("sound family"),
// split by JLPT level and ranked by real usage frequency within each level.
//
// Pipeline (see the project scratchpad for the generating scripts, not part of this repo):
//  1. Kanjium (mifunetoshiro/kanjium, CC-BY-SA-4.0) supplies the phonetic-component grouping
//     (kanjidict.phonetic), each kanji's own JLPT level, onyomi/kunyomi readings and English
//     meaning, and the family's shared reading (the phonetics table).
//  2. scriptin/kanji-frequency (CC-BY-4.0) supplies real usage counts per kanji, summed across
//     its Aozora Bunko, Wikipedia, and Wikinews corpora — the same combine-3-corpora method
//     already used elsewhere in this project (see kanji-freq/rank.js). Each level's families are
//     ranked by the MAX usage count among that level's own member kanji, i.e. by the family's
//     most-used representative a learner at this level will actually run into.
//  3. Tatoeba (tatoeba.org, CC-BY / CC0) supplies one example sentence per kanji where a decent
//     one was found (a real sentence containing a content word that uses that kanji, tokenized
//     and length-filtered the same way the Word Match game's examples were built) — some rarer
//     kanji have no matching sentence and get example: null.
//
// Every level's family list is already in final ranked order (descending by score) — do not
// re-sort client-side. members covers ALL dictionary-wide kanji sharing that phonetic with a
// non-empty JLPT tag (not just this level's own kanji), so a family's tree can show branches
// further along the learner's path (e.g. an N1 relative of an N3 root).
